  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1238 AM EDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nThe front located near our western border will be moving slowly \nacross our western portions of our area through the night, but \nwill be very slow to exit. There will be additional showers and \npossibly a few thunderstorms overnight into Sunday morning near \nand ahead of the front, but the severe threat is waning. A few \nareas have seen flooding this evening, and any areas that see \nrepeated or prolonged heavy rainfall over the next few hours will \ncontinue to be at risk for flooding. This flooding threat is \nexpected to be diminishing later tonight. The south/southeast low \nlevel flow across the eastern mountains will keep gusty winds in \nthe usual higher elevation and foothill areas overnight into early\nSunday, although still do not expect strong enough winds to \nwarrant a wind advisory. These gusty winds will be subsiding \nduring the day as flow weakens and becomes more southwesterly.\n\nAs the atmosphere becomes more unstable ahead of the slow moving \nfront during the day Sunday, a few strong to severe storms will be \npossible mainly over our northeastern areas of NE TN and SW VA where \neffective shear of 25 to 35 kts is forecast along with ML CAPES of \naround 1000 J/kg or more. Damaging winds would be the primary threat \nwith any stronger storms. Heavy rainfall and localized flooding will \nalso be a concern with these storms.  The front will likely edge \neast of our area by late in the day or early Sunday night. \n\nThe models, as is usually the case in these types of scenarios, \nstill are struggling with the exact placement and movement of the \nweak upper low that is left behind and meanders somewhere over the \nregion during the early part of the week. The ensemble approach of \nthe NBM has rather high chances for showers and storms over us for \nMonday through Wednesday, but a minority solution in the models \nsuggests that the upper low drifts off to our east and/or northeast \nmore quickly and chances for precipitation decrease earlier (the \nlatest 00Z NAM is in this camp). We will have to wait for more \nclarity on that, but eventually we will see the chances decrease to \na more typical summertime pattern of scattered showers and storms \naround mainly during the afternoon hours each day by the Thursday \nthrough Saturday time frame if not sooner. We will start the week \nout with high temperatures a bit below normal, but gradual warming \nwill bring temperatures back to around normal by the latter half of \nthe week.",
